@page "/getting-started"

<DocsPage Title="Installation"
          Lead="Add the packages, place their stylesheet components, and put a grid on a page. A MudBlazor application adds the Wrappers and nothing else changes.">

    <DocsSection Title="Requirements">
        <ul>
            <li><b>.NET 10 or newer.</b> The packages target <code>net10.0</code> and load unchanged in newer applications.</li>
            <li><b>Chrome or Edge.</b> They are the supported browsers. Safari and Firefox are out of scope.</li>
            <li><b>An interactive render mode.</b> The grid handles keys, the pointer and the clipboard. It is verified under WebAssembly, and every browser test also runs under Blazor Server.</li>
            <li><b>MudBlazor 9, for the Wrappers.</b> Each MudBlazor Wrapper takes <code>[9.0.0,10.0.0)</code>: 9.0.0 or any later 9.x. An application on MudBlazor 10 is told so at restore (NU1608), rather than left running a Wrapper on a major version it was never tested with.</li>
        </ul>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Install">
        <p>
            The whole family is published to nuget.org as prereleases, at one shared
            <code>0.1.0-beta.N</code> version. Each package depends on exactly the versions of the others
            it was built with, so upgrade them together. Add the products you use, and each one's
            MudBlazor Wrapper in a MudBlazor application. <a href="getting-started/packages">Packages</a> says what
            each one is and which to install. What each version holds, and what it breaks, is in the
            <a href="release-notes">Release notes</a>.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Path="Examples/Setup/install.sh" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Link the stylesheets">
        <p>
            Each package brings one stylesheet, and a component that links it: <code>ExGridStylesheet</code>,
            <code>ExSheetStylesheet</code> and <code>ExPivotStylesheet</code>, and <code>MudExGridStylesheet</code>,
            <code>MudExSheetStylesheet</code> and <code>MudExPivotStylesheet</code> for the Wrappers. You place the
            component, not a <code>&lt;link&gt;</code> to a <code>_content/</code> path, because the address has to
            change whenever the file does (<a href="getting-started#a-new-version-s-files">A new version's files</a>).
            The grid loads its own script module the same way, so there is no script to add.
        </p>
        <p>
            In a Blazor Web App, place them in <code>App.razor</code>'s <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>, where they are
            sent with the page:
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Path="Examples/Setup/web-app-head.html" />
        <p>
            In a standalone WebAssembly application, add them to <code>Program.cs</code> as root components in the
            head, beside <code>HeadOutlet</code>. Not inside a <code>&lt;HeadContent&gt;</code>: only the last
            <code>HeadContent</code> rendered reaches the head, so a page with one of its own would take the
            stylesheets away.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Path="Examples/Setup/standalone-program.cs" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="A first grid">
        <p>
            Hand the grid a source and its columns, and keep both in fields: a new source or a new
            column list on every render would render every row again.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Grid.FirstGrid)" Mud="typeof(ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Grid.FirstGridMud)"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Data/Trade.cs"])" CodeOpen="true" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="In a MudBlazor application">
        <p>
            Set MudBlazor up as usual: its services, its stylesheet and script, and the theme, popover
            and dialog providers in your layout. Then add each package's stylesheet component followed by
            its Wrapper's, as root components in a standalone application, or in <code>App.razor</code>'s
            <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> in a Blazor Web App.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Path="Examples/Setup/Program.cs" />
        <CodeBlock Path="Examples/Setup/MainLayout.razor" />
        <CodeBlock Path="Examples/Setup/host-page-mud.html" />
        <p>
            Put the grid on a <code>MudExGridPaper</code> and give it <code>MudGridChrome</code>. The
            paper brings MudBlazor's surface, palette and type, and follows the theme, dark mode
            included. The Chrome fills the grid's menus, filter panel, find panel and Cell Editor with
            MudBlazor's own controls. The grid's behaviour does not change: switch the site to
            MudBlazor at the top and try the Example above again.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="A new version's files">
        <p>
            The scripts and stylesheets are minified, with source maps. After an upgrade, the browser has to run
            the new script and paint with the new stylesheet beside the new assemblies, on a normal reload, without
            anyone knowing that a hard refresh exists. So each package asks for the files it was built with, at an
            address that changes with the file's content:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <b>In a Blazor Web App</b> on <code>MapStaticAssets</code>, the fingerprinted address .NET gives the
                file, which it serves as immutable.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>In a standalone application</b>, on GitHub Pages or any static host, the plain address with the
                first twelve hex digits of the file's SHA-256 as <code>?v=</code>. A static host that keeps a file
                for ten minutes, as GitHub Pages does, still sends the new one, because it is asked for at a new
                address.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Nothing here is yours to configure, and <code>&lt;ImportMap /&gt;</code> is no longer needed for the
            grid's script.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="A standalone application's first visit">
        <p>
            A standalone application starts its components once the .NET runtime has loaded, and the stylesheet
            components are among them. So on a first visit, and on the first load after an upgrade, a grid can
            stand unstyled for a moment until its stylesheet arrives: about 0.4 s behind a 300 ms round trip,
            measured, and then styled. It is the price of an address the package chooses. A Blazor Web App sends
            the head with the page, so it has no such moment.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="When a page holds another version's files">
        <p>
            When a page still holds another version's files, the products say so instead of looking right and
            being wrong:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <b>"This page holds another version of the grid's script. Reload the page."</b> The grid shows this
                in place of its rows, and says it once to a screen reader, when the script it loaded is not the one
                it was built with: a tab opened before a deploy, a proxy that ignores the address's version, or a
                script that did not load at all. It attaches nothing to that script, so nothing in the grid can be
                selected or typed in, and it still asks your application for its rows. Reloading brings the
                matching files.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>"This page holds another version's stylesheet. Reload the page."</b> A grid, a Sheet or a pivot
                shows this when the stylesheet the page holds belongs to another version of that product, or of
                the MudBlazor Wrapper dressing it: each Wrapper's Chrome declares the stylesheet it brings, and
                the grid or the pivot it dresses shows that one's notice too. The stylesheet decides it on its
                own, with no script: each version's stylesheet shows the notice for any other version and hides
                it for its own. A stylesheet that has not arrived yet shows
                nothing, and one that is missing leaves the grid plainly unstyled.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Both are worded as a refusal is: the built-in Chrome's words, unless someone else words them.
            Replace them through the grid's <code>CommandLabel</code>, a Sheet's <code>CommandLabel</code> or a
            pivot's <code>Label</code>, by the ids in <code>ReloadNoticeLabelIds</code>. A substituted Chrome
            that words refusals through <code>IGridChrome.Words</code>, as the MudBlazor Wrapper does with its
            own <code>Label</code>, words these too.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Behind a remote browser isolation service">
        <p>
            Some organisations route the web through a remote browser isolation service. It runs a site
            in a browser in the cloud and shows the user's own browser a mirror of that page, in which
            none of the site's scripts runs. The grid's script runs in the cloud browser. A key reaches
            the mirror first, and nothing there takes it, so the user's browser acts on it as on any
            page: <b>an arrow key moves the active cell and also scrolls the page</b>, most plainly where
            the grid fills little of it. The grid cannot prevent this from where it runs, and no
            stylesheet can.
        </p>
        <p>
            To tell whether a page is mirrored, open the browser's developer tools on it and run
            <code>typeof Blazor</code> in the Console. On a mirror it answers <code>"undefined"</code>,
            and the Sources panel lists the service's scripts instead of the application's
            <code>_framework</code> and <code>_content</code> files.
        </p>
        <p>
            Which sites are mirrored is the service's policy. Where this was found, the organisation's own
            sites were not. Host the application on a site the service leaves alone, or ask whoever runs
            the service to except its address. This Docs Site is on
            GitHub Pages, so a user behind such a service may see it mirrored.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>
</DocsPage>
